  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Warmer and increasing humidity today, with a chance \nfor a few showers and thunderstorms. \n\nTemperatures will rapidly warm as we march through the morning \nhours. Expect a range of 80s across the region, with a few spots \neven touching 90F in the Genesee Valley during peak heating. We will \nalso see an increase in humidity levels, this as moisture gets fed \nup around the western periphery of the Bermuda high. The combination \nof warming temps and increasing moisture will place heat indexes in \nthe upper 80s to low 90s in the warmest spots this afternoon. \n\nWith increasing moisture and shortwave energy tracking northward we \ncould also see a few afternoon-eve showers and thunderstorms. Given \nPW values ramping up to 1.5-1.7 inches any storms that do form could \nproduce locally heavy down pours. The best chance to see a storm \nwill be across the western Southern Tier and Genesee Valley, with \nlesser chances for the eastern Lake Ontario region. A few showers \nmay linger into this evening and tonight but should weaken with the \nloss of daytime heating. \n  \nKey Message 2...The chance for showers and thunderstorms increases \nThursday and Friday. Any stronger storms may produce locally heavy \ndownpours.  \n\nA shortwave trough and approaching front may bring better coverage \nof showers and thunderstorms on Thursday, with another round on \nFriday. Guidance shows MLCAPE values of +1500 J/kg Thursday but \nrelatively weak shear values (20-30 knots) which 'may' limit the \nsevere weather potential. That said...the main threat at this point \nappears to be localized heavy rainfall. WPC currently has placed all \nof New York in a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall Thursday and \nFriday.",
